<h2>Use Cases: Where Automath Actually Shines</h2>
<h3><strong>1. Formal Verification of Research Mathematics</strong></h3>
<p>Mathematicians increasingly face the &quot;reproducibility crisis&quot; that hit experimental sciences decades ago. Automath demonstrates that even frontier results—Sturmian rigidity, Parry measure characterization, spectral shift formulas—can be machine-verified from first principles. Research groups can fork the repository and establish their own derivation chains with guaranteed audit trails.</p>
<h3><strong>2. Physics Without Physics Axioms</strong></h3>
<p>The project's <strong>physical spacetime skeleton</strong> derivation is genuinely startling. Starting from the forcing framework, it recovers: observer as fiber index, time as decision envelope projection, clock transport curvature δΘ = Ω, and ultimately <strong>Einstein's equation</strong> as the unique minimal second-order covariant closure on the admissible manifold M_adm. No physics axioms were added. This opens a research program asking: <strong>what physics is truly forced by information-theoretic constraints?</strong></p>
<h3><strong>3. Educational Mathematics Reconstruction</strong></h3>
<p>Students learning number theory or symbolic dynamics can trace every theorem back to x² = x + 1. The Zeckendorf bijection, Fibonacci primality fields, Chinese Remainder decomposition—all emerge visibly from combinatorial constraints rather than appearing as arbitrary definitions.</p>
<h3><strong>4. Automated Journal Production</strong></h3>
<p>With 42 papers in pipeline and 3 submission-ready, Automath demonstrates that <strong>AI agents can manage the entire research-to-publication workflow</strong>. This isn't paper mill spam—the results are formally verified, the claims are bounded, and the methodology is transparent.</p>
<h3><strong>5. Cross-Domain Pattern Discovery</strong></h3>
<p>The derivation chain explicitly bridges to established mathematics: de Branges spaces, Ruelle zeta functions, Khintchine's metric theory, gerbe cohomology, Lovelock's theorem. Researchers in one field can discover unexpected connections through the shared forcing framework.</p>
