<h2>FAQ: Your Burning Questions Answered</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Is this actually a proof of the Riemann Hypothesis?</strong>
A: <strong>No.</strong> The project provides a <em>sufficient-condition template</em> translating RH into an auditable positive-definiteness condition. It's a framework for attacking RH, not a claimed proof. The distinction is clearly stated in Section XIII.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How can you have &quot;zero axioms&quot; when you use Mathlib?</strong>
A: Mathlib's definitions are <strong>constructive definitions</strong>, not axioms. Lean 4's core logic (dependent type theory with inductive types) provides the foundation. Everything else is defined, not assumed.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Is the Einstein equation derivation legitimate physics?</strong>
A: The project <strong>explicitly does not claim</strong> full recovery of general relativity. It derives the unique second-order covariant closure on M_adm, which happens to be Einstein's equation. Full physical interpretation requires additional representation and rigidity theorems that are deferred.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Can I contribute without being a Lean 4 expert?</strong>
A: Yes. The Python experiment scripts, documentation, and theory paper LaTeX all accept contributions. The 16-agent pipeline also means formalization assistance is automated—you can propose theorems and let agents attempt verification.</p>
<p><strong>Q: What's the Global Prosperity Open License (GPOL)?</strong>
A: A permissive license requiring <strong>1% net profit contribution if gross revenue exceeds 0.01% of global GDP</strong>. For virtually all users, this is equivalent to free use. It ensures commercial mega-corporations contribute back.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How does this relate to automated theorem proving competitions?</strong>
A: Automath isn't competing on benchmark speed. Its metric is <strong>derivation depth from first principles</strong> and <strong>cross-domain bridge density</strong>. Different optimization target.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Is the live stream actually generating new mathematics?</strong>
A: The stream shows the automated research process—theorem discovery, formalization attempts, agent collaboration. New results emerge continuously, though not every minute is groundbreaking.</p>
